DirtyDecrypt's tie to CVE-2026-31635 is an attribution by Will Dormann and the NVD record's link to the proof of concept, not the discoverers' own: the kernel's description of CVE-2026-31635 is an inverted length check that lets oversized RESPONSE authenticators reach rxgk_decrypt_skb() and hit a BUG_ON, scored CVSS 3.1 7.5 as a network denial of service ([ENISA EUVD-2026-25528](https://euvd.enisa.europa.eu/enisa/eu_vulnerability_database/EUVD-2026-25528); [BleepingComputer, 2026-05-18](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/exploit-available-for-new-dirtydecrypt-linux-root-escalation-flaw/); [The Hacker News, 2026-05-19](https://thehackernews.com/2026/05/dirtydecrypt-poc-released-for-linux.html)). The affected range is kernels from 6.16 before 6.18.23 or 6.19.13, with 7.0 also fixed, and older kernels fall outside it; no source says Debian Stable, RHEL or Ubuntu LTS are unaffected ([ENISA EUVD-2026-25528](https://euvd.enisa.europa.eu/enisa/eu_vulnerability_database/EUVD-2026-25528)). V12's proof of concept was already public when BleepingComputer reported it on 2026-05-18 ([BleepingComputer, 2026-05-18](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/exploit-available-for-new-dirtydecrypt-linux-root-escalation-flaw/)). The interim mitigation BleepingComputer gives blocks the esp4, esp6 and rxrpc modules and breaks IPsec VPNs and AFS ([BleepingComputer, 2026-05-18](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/exploit-available-for-new-dirtydecrypt-linux-root-escalation-flaw/)).
